"""Quiet-month columns: no invented local statistics, outcomes or named sources."""

FEATURES = {
"FEATURE_DISTRICT_PORTRAIT": {
"heads": ["A district is more than its boundary", "How to look at a neighbourhood", "The view from ordinary life", "What makes a place belong to people"],
"sub": "A portrait of {district}, considered through the lives a place must accommodate.",
"pieces": [
"A district can be described as a boundary on a plan. That is useful if the question is where it ends. It is less useful if the question is what it means to live there. For this portrait of {district}, begin with the second question.\n\nImagine a resident trying to explain home to someone who knows only the colony's public map. The explanation might begin with a route to work, a familiar view or the feeling of knowing where one belongs. None would fit neatly into a boundary line.\n\nA good portrait should leave room for different answers. A place can be convenient to one person and difficult to another, loved for something its neighbour scarcely notices. The most revealing question is not which description can silence the others, but what they allow us to understand together.",
"What should a newspaper mean when it calls {district} a community? The word can be warm, and warmth can be a way of avoiding a harder question. People need a place in which to live, not merely a flattering description of the place.\n\nConsider how a portrait changes with its point of view. A planner might ask about connections. Someone seeking a home might ask about belonging. A person thinking about work might begin with the journey. These are ways of looking, not a claim that every resident has the same priorities.\n\nThe useful portrait is the one that does not pretend a district has a single voice. A boundary can contain competing hopes. Understanding a neighbourhood means taking those hopes seriously without reducing them to a pleasant phrase for the newspaper's quieter pages.",
"To look at {district} only as a location is to miss the question a portrait ought to ask: what kind of daily life should a place make possible? It is an ordinary question, which is one reason it deserves space.\n\nImagine taking a visitor through a neighbourhood without beginning with its most impressive claim. Begin instead with what a resident would need to explain: how a place feels familiar, what makes it easy or awkward to inhabit, and why different people might answer differently.\n\nA district's reputation should not be allowed to answer on everyone's behalf. Admiration and complaint can each reveal something, provided neither is mistaken for the whole. This paper's interest is in the distance between a place as it is described and a place as someone must actually live in it.",
"There is an attractive temptation in a district portrait: to find a phrase that explains everyone. Resist it. {district} deserves an account that can admit more than a single kind of attachment, more than a single idea of what makes a place good.\n\nThink of the difference between choosing somewhere to live and making a life there. A location may be judged for its practical uses, while belonging is a less tidy matter. A portrait ought to have room for both, without assuming that one person's priorities settle the question for another.\n\nThe most useful question for a reader is therefore a personal one: what would you want a newspaper to notice about the place you call home? The answer is a better beginning than a slogan, and a useful restraint on the writer who wants a neat ending."
],
"voices": [
"The gazette offers this portrait as an invitation to careful observation. Administrative descriptions have a purpose, but they should not be mistaken for a complete account of the people and purposes a district must accommodate.",
"Our movement believes belonging should have political meaning. A district is not simply a convenient collection of potential supporters; it is a place whose ordinary life ought to matter to the convictions we bring into public argument.",
"Labour's portrait begins with the people who must fit work into life and life around work. A neighbourhood deserves to be judged from that position as well as from the comfort of an attractive description.",
"For a commercial reader, a district is also a place where practical needs meet decisions. A useful portrait should encourage attention to those needs without pretending that a reputation is a market survey or a forecast.",
"A neighbourhood is not obliged to look impressive in a headline. The interesting question is whether a person can call it home. Leave the grand phrase alone for a moment and give that smaller question some room."
]},

"FEATURE_LETTERS": {
"heads": ["Letters: the ordinary standard", "Readers ask for a little perspective", "From the letters desk: daily life", "Letters on promises, work and belonging"],
"sub": "Three reader opinions on the everyday purposes of the colony.",
"pieces": [
"To the editor: I would like our public arguments to be judged by an ordinary day. Can a proposal be explained to someone trying to live decently, without requiring a speech before the explanation? I am not asking politics to be simple. I am asking it to remember its purpose.\n\nTo the editor: Work ought to leave room for a life. I do not think that is an extravagant principle, though it can sound like one when the argument becomes sufficiently grand. A decent standard should be intelligible from the point of view of the person expected to meet it.\n\nTo the editor: Belonging should mean more than being told to feel proud. I would rather hear a serious discussion of what we owe each other than another elegant description of how fortunate people ought to feel.",
"To the editor: A promise is not made better by becoming harder to understand. If an idea is worth defending, its purpose should survive a plain explanation. I would welcome a little less admiration for the performance and a little more interest in the obligation the words are meant to express.\n\nTo the editor: Please keep asking whose time a proposal requires. Time belongs to ordinary people too. A plan can sound convenient from the position of its author and rather less convenient from the position of someone expected to fit a working life around it.\n\nTo the editor: I do not want every disagreement to become a test of whether someone loves the colony. It ought to be possible to care about a place and still want it to be better.",
"To the editor: I am suspicious of any argument that begins by explaining why ordinary needs are too small to matter. The colony's future should be large enough to contain a decent daily life. Otherwise I am not sure whose future the speaker has in mind.\n\nTo the editor: Fairness is easy to admire when it asks nothing of the admirer. I would like political principles to be tested at the uncomfortable point, where they require us to take someone else's position seriously. That seems a better measure than the warmth of a slogan.\n\nTo the editor: A newspaper should leave room for a quiet subject. There is value in thinking about how we live together without first finding someone to blame. Please consider that an opinion, rather than an instruction to make the paper dull.",
"To the editor: A good public decision should be able to answer the question, 'What is this for?' I do not mean that every answer must please me. I mean that the purpose should be visible, and that the people asked to accept a decision deserve to hear it.\n\nTo the editor: I would rather be treated as a neighbour with an opinion than an audience waiting to applaud. Belonging to a colony should leave room for both loyalty and disagreement. The disagreement may even be one of the ways in which loyalty becomes useful.\n\nTo the editor: Please resist the idea that being practical means having no principles. It should mean taking principles seriously enough to ask how they enter daily life. A conviction that never reaches that question seems rather comfortable for its owner."
],
"voices": [
"The gazette prints these views as opinions. Their value lies in the questions they put to public life, and their appearance here should not be taken as an administrative finding or a statement of official policy.",
"Our letters page should be a place where convictions meet ordinary expectations. These opinions invite a serious political answer; a party worthy of support should not need every reader to express the same thought in the same language.",
"The labour letters desk welcomes an argument that begins with everyday life. Solidarity does not require identical opinions, but it does require that the person behind an opinion be treated as someone whose needs deserve attention.",
"The correspondence offers judgments rather than a survey. Commercial readers should value the questions without treating the letters as a numerical account of public opinion. A useful argument need not pretend to be a measurement to deserve space.",
"No great office is required to have an opinion about daily life. These letters make their point without a grand title. Readers are invited to argue with the ideas, preferably before arguing with an imagined author."
]},

"FEATURE_SHIP_DAY": {
"heads": ["Before the ship: a study in waiting", "The port and the idea of arrival", "What a distant route asks of patience", "The weeks before a ship is due"],
"sub": "A quiet-month reflection on the port before a ship's expected arrival.",
"pieces": [
"The weeks before a ship is due invite a particular way of looking at a port. It is a place defined by movement, considered at a moment when the interesting movement is still a prospect. Waiting becomes part of the subject.\n\nImagine looking towards the route and trying to separate what is known from what is hoped for. An expected vessel can stand for all sorts of wishes. That does not make every imagined cargo real, or turn an anticipated arrival into a promise to satisfy every need.\n\nThe useful portrait is therefore about patience as much as transport. A distant connection gives the colony something to think about beyond itself, while the work of living remains local. The question for the observer is what an arrival can reasonably mean, and what we are merely asking it to mean.",
"A port is an edge with a purpose. In the weeks before a ship is due, that purpose can be considered without pretending the vessel has already arrived. The colony's connection to the home world is both a route and an idea about distance.\n\nConsider the difference between expecting transport and expecting transformation. The first belongs to the practical business of a voyage. The second is a much larger claim, liable to attach itself to whatever someone would most like an arrival to bring. A newspaper should be careful not to confuse them.\n\nThis is a portrait of anticipation rather than a cargo account. It asks how a colony imagines its place in a wider relationship, and how easily a sensible expectation can acquire an unreasonable burden of hope. The ship should not have to carry the writer's inventions as well.",
"What is a port like before an expected ship? The interesting answer need not be a list of things on a apron. It can begin with the difference between a place used for departures and arrivals and the waiting that makes those events meaningful.\n\nImagine a conversation about the coming voyage. What would count as a reasonable expectation? What belongs to hope? Those questions matter because a distant route is not an unlimited promise, however attractive the thought of an arrival may be.\n\nThe colony's daily life cannot be postponed until a vessel appears. That is a useful perspective for any portrait of anticipation: the present remains a place to inhabit. A ship may be the subject of attention without becoming a fictional answer to every question the colony can ask about its future.",
"Waiting for a ship is a good subject for a newspaper, provided the writer does not begin unloading it in advance. An expected arrival is an occasion to think about the colony's connection with the home world, not permission to invent the contents of a hold.\n\nConsider the port as a meeting between local life and a distant route. It gives a practical form to the idea that the colony belongs to a wider relationship. Before a ship is due, that relationship can be contemplated as an expectation rather than described as an accomplished exchange.\n\nThere is a quiet discipline in keeping the two apart. Hope deserves a place in a portrait, but not the disguise of a manifest. A useful account allows readers to think about distance without asking them to believe the voyage has brought something not yet established."
],
"voices": [
"The gazette distinguishes an expected arrival from an arrival notice. This reflection concerns the period of anticipation, and should not be read as a declaration of a particular launch date, cargo or administrative arrangement for a voyage.",
"Our political interest is in the purposes a connection serves. The colony's relationship with the home world should be considered through common needs and responsibilities, rather than reduced either to admiration for distance or resentment of it.",
"Labour asks how the distant connection fits the lives of ordinary people. A portrait of waiting should leave room for that question, without assuming that a ship's approach guarantees a particular benefit to the person doing the waiting.",
"Commercial patience requires a distinction between expectation and delivery. The period before a voyage is a useful reminder that a prospective arrival is not stock on hand, a completed transaction or a basis for inventing a commercial forecast.",
"Look towards the route if you like. Just do not let the imagination unload a perfect future before the vessel is here. Waiting is interesting enough without pretending that a ship can carry an answer to everything."
]},

"FEATURE_SEASON": {
"heads": ["Living inside a season", "The calendar and the ordinary day", "A seasonal view of the colony", "What the season asks us to notice"],
"sub": "A reflection on {season} and the habit of making a life on this planet.",
"pieces": [
"A season is a name on the calendar and a way of paying attention. In {colony}, the subject is {season}. A portrait of it need not pretend that the name alone supplies a forecast or tells every resident what the day feels like.\n\nConsider how weather enters an ordinary decision. It is something to think about when work, a journey or a field is the subject. Different people can encounter the same season as different practical questions. The useful writer remembers that variety instead of inventing a single mood for the whole colony.\n\nSeasonal colour should not be an excuse for false precision. A newspaper can invite readers to look at the world around them without announcing rain, a harvest or a change in temperature it has not established. The season deserves observation before it is fitted to a convenient description.",
"The calendar calls the season {season}. That is a beginning for a feature, rather than a complete picture of the weather in {colony}. A season becomes meaningful through the ordinary business of inhabiting it: the questions people bring to work, movement and the land.\n\nImagine asking what someone would notice first. A person considering a field might look differently from a person considering a journey. Neither point of view has to be dismissed to make room for the other. A good seasonal portrait should be able to keep both in mind.\n\nThere is a temptation to make the planet behave like a familiar phrase. Resist it. The subject is the colony's relationship with its surroundings, not the writer's wish for an easy opening about the weather. A careful look is more interesting than a borrowed certainty.",
"What does it mean to notice {season}? One answer is to stop treating the environment as scenery around the real business of the colony. Daily life takes place somewhere, and that somewhere deserves attention even when no dramatic event has made it news.\n\nA seasonal feature can ask what people would need to consider in their work or their plans. It should not claim to know a particular day's conditions from the calendar alone. The distinction matters: a name is useful, but it is not a measurement or a report from a field.\n\nThe most honest portrait leaves room for the reader's own observation. Look at the season as a setting for practical life, with different interests bringing different questions. There is enough to think about without inventing a flourishing crop or a sudden chill to give the paragraph a flourish.",
"A season can be a practical matter before it becomes an attractive subject for a column. In writing about {season}, begin with the idea that weather and the land belong to the conditions of daily life, rather than to the newspaper's supply of decorative phrases.\n\nConsider the questions a portrait should ask. What would matter to someone thinking about a field? What would matter to someone thinking about a journey or a day's work? These are points of view, not an assertion that the season has produced a particular result.\n\nThe colony inhabits a planet, and a useful feature should make that thought feel ordinary rather than grand. We can pay attention without pretending the calendar supplies facts it does not contain. The invitation is to observe the surroundings carefully, not to applaud a description before asking whether it fits."
],
"voices": [
"The gazette treats the calendar and a weather observation as distinct records. This seasonal reflection supplies no forecast or account of agricultural conditions; it offers a way to consider the surroundings in which the colony's ordinary affairs take place.",
"Our movement should remember that political life takes place under material conditions. Attention to a season can be an attention to people's needs, provided it does not turn the landscape into an excuse for forgetting responsibility or abandoning principle.",
"Labour's seasonal question begins with people doing the work. Conditions deserve attention because lives are lived in them. A picturesque description should never be allowed to make the practical demands of a working day disappear from the reader's view.",
"For a commercial reader, the discipline is to distinguish seasonal attention from an output forecast. A calendar label does not establish a crop yield, a cost or a delivery condition. Practical judgment begins with the circumstances actually observed.",
"The season does not owe the newspaper a dramatic sky. There is interest in an ordinary look around, if the writer can resist inventing a storm or a perfect harvest to make the page feel more exciting."
]},

"FEATURE_FOUNDERS": {
"heads": ["Looking back without polishing the past", "The founding years and the uses of memory", "A beginning is not a finished argument", "Remembering why a colony begins"],
"sub": "The founding years deserve reflection without an invented golden age.",
"pieces": [
"Looking back at the founding years of {colony} is an invitation to think about beginnings. It is also an invitation to a familiar mistake: making the past conveniently agree with whatever one wants to argue about the present.\n\nA founding is a beginning, not a complete answer to every question that follows. The colony's origin deserves attention without being turned into a claim that its later choices were already settled. Memory should open a discussion about purpose, rather than close it with an invented account of unanimous intention.\n\nConsider the standard a useful retrospective ought to meet. It should distinguish an attachment to the beginning from a claim to know every person's hopes. It should allow the present to ask questions of the past, without asking the past to supply a flattering verdict on the present writer's politics.",
"The founding years can become a very comfortable place for an argument to hide. Everyone is invited to admire the beginning; the difficult question is what that admiration asks of life in {colony} now. A retrospective should have room for the question.\n\nThere is a difference between honouring a common undertaking and announcing what every founder would have wanted. The first can be a serious political and human act. The second requires knowledge a pleasant phrase cannot supply. A newspaper should not invent a consensus merely because it would make the ending more satisfying.\n\nThink of the colony's beginning as a reason to examine responsibility. What does it mean to continue a place rather than simply praise its origin? That is an opinion worth discussing, and it needs neither a fabricated hardship nor a fictional speech to make it matter.",
"A beginning attracts a story. In looking back at the founding years of {colony}, the useful discipline is to remember that a good story is not entitled to add facts merely because they feel suitable to the occasion.\n\nThe past can be admired without being made perfect. It can be questioned without being dismissed. Those are judgments about how to remember, not claims about a particular decision or a named person's purpose. A retrospective becomes more useful when it leaves room for that distinction.\n\nThe question for readers is what a founding should mean to the work of living together. Pride may be part of the answer. Responsibility should be part of it too. A colony cannot live entirely inside the account of how it began; the beginning matters partly because there is a present in which to consider it.",
"What do we ask of the founding years when we look back? Sometimes the question is about belonging. Sometimes it is about purpose. The newspaper's task is to allow those questions without filling the past with people and episodes it has not established.\n\nA colony's origin can provide a language of common effort. It should not become an instruction to agree about every later choice. People can value a beginning while arguing seriously about the obligations that follow from it. The disagreement need not make the attachment less real.\n\nThis retrospective therefore proposes a modest standard for memory: use it to think, rather than to avoid thinking. The founding of {colony} deserves more than a polished phrase placed beyond examination. It deserves the attention we give to something whose meaning can still require an honest conversation among the people who inherit it."
],
"voices": [
"The gazette distinguishes reflection from a historical entry. This column records no additional episode or attribution from the founding years. Its subject is the care with which a common beginning should be considered in the colony's public life.",
"Our principles should be strong enough to face a past that does not supply every answer. The founding is not a private possession of whichever political argument invokes it most loudly. Its meaning deserves a serious and open contest of convictions.",
"Labour's interest in a beginning is the common undertaking it can represent. Remembering a colony should leave room for ordinary work and shared responsibility, without requiring invented heroes before the people who sustain a place can deserve attention.",
"An origin is not a business plan for every subsequent year. Commercial readers can value a founding while distinguishing its symbolic importance from the decisions a living colony must still make. Memory should inform judgment rather than replace it.",
"The past need not shine like a polished trophy to deserve attention. A beginning can matter without a perfect speech attached to it. Readers are entitled to remember the colony without being sold an imaginary golden age."
]},

"FEATURE_MARKET": {
"heads": ["A market walk without a price list", "What a price is meant to tell us", "The market from the buyer's end", "Looking beyond the attractive display"],
"sub": "A reflective walk through the market, with attention to cost, need and availability.",
"pieces": [
"A walk through the market is a good way to think about the distance between wanting something and being able to obtain it. The useful questions are plain: what is dear, what is cheap, and what cannot be had? The questions do not supply their own answers.\n\nConsider the buyer's point of view. A price has meaning in relation to a need and the means of meeting it. Something can look attractive in a display and still fail that practical test. A portrait of the market should keep the distinction visible rather than substitute enthusiasm for an account of affordability.\n\nThis is an invitation to look carefully, not a list of current bargains in {colony}. A good market walk should leave the reader more attentive to the relationship between cost and daily life, without pretending the writer can announce a price or a shortage from the idea of a market alone.",
"What makes something cheap? The question sounds simple until the person asking it has a particular need to meet. A market walk should be capable of looking at the same offer from more than the seller's position. Price is a relation, not merely an attractive word.\n\nImagine following a buyer's questions rather than the shape of a display. Is the thing needed? Is it obtainable? What does its cost mean for the person considering it? Those are useful ways of looking without inventing a basket, a wage or a current price for the colony.\n\nThe market deserves a portrait that connects exchange to ordinary life. Admiration for commerce need not require the buyer to disappear. Nor does concern for need require a fictional shortage. There is interest in the practical judgment itself, if the writer resists the temptation to supply answers that have not been established.",
"The most revealing part of a market can be the question someone brings to it. A person looking for a necessity and a person considering a pleasant extra need not see the same offer in the same way. A useful portrait should leave room for both positions.\n\nThis walk through the idea of the market begins with availability. What cannot be had is a different question from what is costly, and a low price is not useful for a thing that cannot actually be obtained. The distinctions matter without requiring a made-up example from a stall.\n\nFor readers in {colony}, the invitation is to connect the commercial language to the practical need it is supposed to serve. A market is not fully understood by admiring the possibility of exchange. The interesting question is how an exchange looks from the life of the person who needs to make it.",
"A market portrait should not make every reader into the same customer. People can approach an offer with different purposes, means and priorities. The question is how to look at that variety without announcing a collection of local prices the newspaper does not possess.\n\nBegin with the distinction between cost and value. Something may deserve admiration and still be the wrong purchase for a particular need. Something plain may be exactly what is wanted. These are judgments a market invites; they are not a claim about a particular item on sale in {colony}.\n\nA walk worth writing about therefore pays attention to decisions, not just displays. Ask what is dear, what is cheap and what cannot be had, then remember that each answer needs observation. The reader should leave with sharper questions rather than a fictional shopping list dressed up as a report from the colony's commercial life."
],
"voices": [
"The gazette distinguishes this reflection from a current price notice. No particular offer or supply condition is recorded here. The subject is the care with which availability and cost should be considered when describing the market's place in daily life.",
"Our political principles should make sense at the buyer's end of an exchange. A market is part of the colony's life, and its purposes deserve argument without requiring either a celebration of every price or an accusation unsupported by the record.",
"Labour asks what a pay packet can mean in practice. Cost and availability matter because a household has needs to meet. A market portrait should begin with that ordinary connection, rather than treating working people as an afterthought to the display.",
"Commercial judgment distinguishes asking price, value and availability. The market is best understood by attending to those particulars rather than inventing a general mood from its existence. A buyer's practical questions are part of commerce, not an interruption of it.",
"A display can look splendid and still leave the important question unanswered: can someone get what they need? Keep looking from that end of the matter. A market story deserves more than a writer admiring something without knowing its price."
]},

"FEATURE_ASSEMBLY_SKETCH": {
"heads": ["In praise of an undramatic Assembly day", "Public work without a grand scene", "The Assembly beyond the great speech", "A dull day can still have a purpose"],
"sub": "A sketch of the ordinary idea of parliamentary work, without an invented debate.",
"pieces": [
"A dull day in the Colonial Assembly is a tempting subject for a joke. It is also a useful occasion to ask what we expect public work to look like. Not every worthwhile political activity should have to arrive with the shape of a dramatic scene.\n\nImagine watching parliamentary business without waiting for the perfect speech. The interesting question might be whether a purpose is clear, whether an argument can be followed, and whether responsibility remains visible when the performance is less exciting. These are standards for observation, not a report of a particular exchange.\n\nThis sketch supplies no invented motion, vote or remark from a member. It asks instead whether a newspaper can pay attention to an institution without manufacturing a spectacle. Readers deserve politics worth following, but worth following and entertaining are not necessarily the same judgment about the business of a colony.",
"What would make a dull Assembly day worth noticing? Begin with the idea that public authority ought to be intelligible even when it is not exciting. A legislature should not have to produce a grand scene before a newspaper can consider its purpose.\n\nPicture the observer's task as an exercise in attention. Look for the relationship between a question and the responsibility of answering it. Ask whether the language helps someone understand the matter. None of that requires the writer to invent a quotation or a vote to give the sketch a tidy ending.\n\nThe Colonial Assembly can be considered as a place of public work, rather than merely a stage for its most memorable moments. That is the modest argument of this feature. Boredom is not proof of merit, but the absence of spectacle should not be treated as proof that nothing worth thinking about can remain.",
"A newspaper likes a moment that can be put in a headline. The Colonial Assembly has a larger purpose than supplying such moments. A sketch of a dull day is an opportunity to remember the difference between political work and the performance that makes work conspicuous.\n\nConsider what an interested citizen should be able to ask. What is the matter for decision? Who must answer for a position? How does an argument connect to the colony's life? Those are ordinary questions, and they do not become less useful when the setting is undramatic.\n\nThere is no fictional parliamentary exchange in this sketch. The point is to look at an institution without asking imagination to furnish a scandal. Readers can demand clarity and purpose from public life while allowing that some of the attention it requires may be quiet, patient and less suitable for a triumphant quotation.",
"The dull day is a small test for a newspaper's conception of politics. If the only possible subject is a clash, the writer may be tempted to provide one. The better question is whether ordinary parliamentary work can deserve attention on its own terms.\n\nImagine looking at the Colonial Assembly as an institution to be understood rather than an entertainment to be reviewed. The observer might ask how public purposes become arguments, and what makes those arguments answerable to the people outside. That approach needs patience, but it does not need an invented speech.\n\nThis feature leaves the day's motions, votes and personalities unspecified. Its sketch is an invitation to consider the purpose of a legislature when no dramatic event is being announced. A public institution should be able to matter without performing a crisis for the convenience of the newspaper that wants to write about it."
],
"voices": [
"The gazette regards clear procedure and clear explanation as proper objects of public attention. This sketch is not a record of proceedings. It offers a way to consider parliamentary work without attributing an unreported act to the Assembly.",
"Our movement believes conviction belongs in the routine of public responsibility as well as in the great speech. A political principle should remain recognisable when there is no dramatic occasion to make its performance especially convenient or attractive.",
"Labour wants an Assembly capable of remembering the people outside it. A dull day is no excuse for forgetting work and daily life, just as an exciting day is no guarantee that those subjects have received serious attention.",
"For managers, public work deserves judgment by purpose and conduct rather than theatrical quality. A quiet institution is not automatically an efficient one, but a good performance is not a measure of administrative value either. Attention should remain practical.",
"No grand row has been supplied for this sketch. Readers are invited to survive the disappointment and ask what the Assembly is for. A dull page might still contain a worthwhile question, if the newspaper stops trying to manufacture a villain."
]},

"FEATURE_WORK": {
"heads": ["A day's work, considered from the inside", "The person behind the useful job", "Work is a life as well as an output", "Following the ordinary purpose of a job"],
"sub": "A quiet-month reflection on the people whose work makes a colony possible.",
"pieces": [
"A day with the people who do a job should begin with the people, not with an impressive description of the output. Consider the baker as a subject for a feature. The interest is not simply the idea of bread, but the human responsibility involved in useful work.\n\nThis is an invitation to consider the job, not an account of a named bakery in {colony}. No particular shift, wage or production figure is being reported. The useful question is what it means to perform work that someone else needs, and how easily the worker can disappear behind the thing produced.\n\nA portrait should leave room for skill, effort and the life around the job. Admiring a useful occupation need not mean making its demands invisible. The person who does the work deserves to be considered as more than the answer to somebody else's need.",
"A useful way to think about a day of work is to follow its purpose. Consider nursing: the subject is care, and the person doing the job should not disappear into an attractive phrase about dedication. A portrait ought to allow a human life on both sides of the work.\n\nThis feature does not report a particular patient, workplace or shift in the colony. It asks what a reader should notice when considering an occupation whose purpose matters to others. Skill and responsibility deserve attention without an invented emergency to make the story feel important.\n\nThe ordinary day is enough of a subject. A job can be admired while its conditions remain a legitimate question, and a worker can be valued without being required to become a hero first. That is a useful standard for a newspaper trying to write about the people who do necessary work.",
"Consider a driver's day as a question about responsibility. A journey is often described from the position of the person who wants to arrive or receive something. A feature about work should also have room for the person expected to make that movement possible.\n\nNo particular route, load or vehicle is being reported here. The subject is the perspective a portrait ought to take. A job can appear simple from its useful result, while the person doing it deserves attention as someone exercising judgment rather than merely supplying movement.\n\nA newspaper need not invent an exciting incident to make ordinary work worth considering. The more useful question is what respectful attention looks like: enough interest in the task to understand its purpose, and enough interest in the worker to remember that the task belongs within a whole life, not the other way around.",
"A builder is a good subject for thinking about the difference between a plan and the work required to make something real. The finished purpose can attract all the attention. A portrait of a working day should look at the person as well as the promise.\n\nThis is not a report from a particular building site in {colony}. It supplies no task list, wage or account of conditions. Instead, it asks what a reader should keep in view when thinking about an occupation whose result can be easier to admire than the human effort behind it.\n\nWork deserves attention without an invented achievement to make the feature grand. The job has a purpose; the person has a life. A serious portrait should be able to respect both, without making decent conditions seem like an inconvenient footnote to the thing somebody else wants completed."
],
"voices": [
"The gazette offers this occupational reflection without attributing specific conditions to a local employer. The purpose is to consider the responsibilities of useful work while distinguishing general observation from an account of a particular workplace or its arrangements.",
"Our principles should be visible in the way we think about a working day. A political movement worthy of support should treat the person doing a useful job as part of its purpose, rather than a convenient symbol for a speech.",
"Labour's standard is a decent life for the person doing the work. Usefulness is a reason for respect, not an argument that conditions can be ignored. A job belongs in a life; solidarity should never lose sight of that order.",
"A useful output depends on work, and a sound management view should keep the person and the task in the same account. Respect for skill and responsibility need not be treated as a departure from practical commercial judgment.",
"No invented hero, no perfect quotation, no dramatic rescue of the day. Just a useful job and a person who deserves attention. The ordinary working life ought to be interesting enough before a newspaper begins improving it for effect."
]},
}

def feature(request, index):
    entry = FEATURES[request["event"]]
    flavour = ("STATE", "PARTY", "UNION", "BUSINESS", "TABLOID").index(request["flavour"])
    return dict(headline=entry["heads"][index], sub=entry["sub"], body=entry["pieces"][index] + "\n\n" + entry["voices"][flavour])
